# SwiftFix Production Rules

## Tech Stack
* Framework: Next.js (App Router, TypeScript)
* UI/Styling: Tailwind CSS, Google Stitch/MCP generated components, Lucide-react Icons
* Backend/API: Next.js Route Handlers (Serverless API)
* Database: PostgreSQL via Prisma ORM
* Core APIs: Gemini API (AI requests), Privy (Auth/Wallet), TinyFish (Web Scraper), Paystack API (Fiat Withdrawals & Account Resolution)

## UI & Layout Standards
* Global Layout: Dark sidebar navigation on the left, white/light main content area on the right.
* Responsiveness: Must be fully mobile-responsive. Sidebar must collapse into a hamburger menu on small screens.
* Components: Use modular, reusable UI components. Never write inline CSS. Use Tailwind classes.

## Coding Standards & Architecture
* Always write fully typed TypeScript code. Do not use 'any'.
* Keep React components small. Separate business logic (custom hooks) from UI components.
* Server/Client boundaries: Clearly mark client components with "use client" at the top when using hooks or interactivity.
* Use async/await for all asynchronous operations.

## Financial & Edge Case Handling (CRITICAL)
* Currency Swaps (USDT <-> NGN) and Withdrawals MUST be wrapped in a Prisma `$transaction` to prevent partial balance updates if the server crashes.
* Always verify the user has sufficient balance before initiating a swap or withdrawal. Show a clear UI error if funds are insufficient.
* For Paystack withdrawals, ALWAYS call the "Resolve Account Number" endpoint first. Do not allow the user to click "Withdraw" until the account name is verified and displayed on the screen.
* External API Failures: If Gemini, TinyFish, or Paystack APIs time out or fail, catch the error gracefully and return a user-friendly message (e.g., "Our search network is currently busy, please try again"). Do not let the app crash.

## Security & State
* Never hardcode API keys or secrets. Always read from process.env.
* Assume the user's base currency toggle defaults to NGN (Naira), with USDT as the secondary option.